'''Ngarla''' is a [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan language]] of coastal [[Western Australia]]. It is possibly [[mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]] with [[Panyjima language|Panyjima]] and [[Martuthunira language|Martuthunira]], but the three are considered distinct languages.

Ngarla is a member of the [[Ngayarda languages|Ngayarda]] branch of the [[Pama–Nyungan languages]]. Dench (1995) believed there was insufficient data to enable it to be confidently classified, but Bowern & Koch (2004) include it without proviso.

Ngarla is spoken near [[Port Hedland]]. The "Ngarla" on the [[Ashburton River (Western Australia)|Ashburton River]] is a dialect of a different, though possibly related, language, [[Yinhawangka language|Yinhawangka]].

According to the [[Irra Wangga Language Centre]], "Ngarla is no longer spoken today, although there remain some community members who know some words and phrases in the language".<ref name=irra/>

